    "id": "p6-problem-3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Explain why the equation $|x|=-1-x^2$ has no solution, without explicitly solving for them.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$|x|\\geq0$, whereas $-1-x^2\\leq-1<0$, so the sides cannot be equal.",
      "worked": "For every real $x$, $|x|$ is nonnegative. Also, $x^2\\geq0$, so $-1-x^2\\leq-1$ is negative. Therefore the two sides cannot be equal, and there is no real solution.",
